WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 66. It's June 9th, 2026.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.940
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.620
So imagine your phone could actually chat with you instead of just following one command at a time.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.100
That is basically what Apple just announced with a new version of Siri called Siri AI.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.380
The goal is to make it feel like a real back and forth conversation rather than barking orders at a robot.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:34.780
Think of the old Siri like a strict librarian who only answers exact questions you write on a slip of

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.240
paper. The new version is more like talking to a helpful friend who remembers what you said two

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:46.300
minutes ago and can follow up naturally. It will use a mix of small AI models that run right on your

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:52.220
iPhone and bigger models that live in the cloud when the task gets tough. This matters because most

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:58.260
of us already ask our phones for quick help with timers or texts every single day. A version that

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.660
handles follow up questions could make homework or planning your day feel much smoother without

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:08.280
switching apps. For you personally, it could turn your phone into a steadier helper for school projects

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.300
or creative ideas. The new features are planned to arrive this fall so you can test them yourself soon

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.980
if you have an iPhone. To see how far the current version goes right now just open regular Siri

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:26.520
and ask a follow up like what did we just talk about? Okay now from my favorite part of the show where

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.640
we look under the hood. You know how your phone sometimes uses its own battery power for simple

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.660
things like showing the time but switches to Wi-Fi when it needs something bigger from the internet.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:43.660
Now picture the same idea but for thinking instead of power. Step one a small AI model lives right

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:50.260
on your phone and handles quick basic tasks without sending anything anywhere. Step two when the question

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.780
gets harder the phone quietly asks a much larger AI model sitting on big servers in the cloud

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:02.880
to take over. Step three the phone decides which one to use based on how complex the request seems

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.620
just like it picks Wi-Fi over cellular to save data when both are available.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.280
And that is basically what on device versus cloud AI models are doing when they split the work

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:20.940
between your phone and remote servers. So next time someone says hybrid AI architecture you can tell them

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.140
it is basically your phone choosing between its own brain and a bigger one in the cloud depending on

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:32.440
the job. Not so scary right? Now let's check out some cool stuff you can try yourself. Apple also

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.980
updated its built in image generator called image playground so it can now create better and more

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.840
useful pictures from simple text descriptions. Think of it like upgrading from basic crayons that

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.980
only make simple shapes to a full art kit that handles details and style without looking off.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.660
This matters if you like doodling ideas for school projects memes or just fun visuals.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:01.000
You can find it inside the image playground app on recent iPhones or iPads. Try this right now

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.880
open the app and type something like a cartoon version of my favorite video game character exploring

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:13.240
a rainy city at night. See how the updated model handles the details and mood. Here are two quick

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:19.800
bits that caught my eye. First andthropic shared a post explaining why AI got good at coding before

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:26.120
it got good at biology research. The reason is that biology databases were never designed for AI to

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.040
read them easily. Think of old city streets built for horses that now have to handle cars and delivery

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:39.000
robots. The post asks how we can redesign those databases so future AI helpers can actually use them.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:45.180
Second a recent discussion pointed out that building an AI agent is only about 20 percent

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.400
about the smart model itself. The other 80 percent is setting up clear rules for who gets notified

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:57.420
how mistakes get fixed and where information gets stored. It is the everyday process stuff that

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:08.840
turns a cool demo into something that actually helps in real life. And that's a wrap. If any of

